\documentclass{article}
\title{Momentum Contrast for Visual Representations}
\author{Jane Doe}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
In recent years contrastive learning is kinda popular in computer vision. Lots of researchers use it to pretrain models without labels, and it works pretty well. The idea is simple: you take an image, make two different versions of it, and push the model to treat them as the same thing while telling it that other images are different. People have found this trick gives features that transfer nicely to downstream tasks like detection and segmentation. But there are still annoying problems. The batch size thing is a big deal because small batches make the results much worse, and training takes forever. Also, nobody really knows why some augmentations help a lot and others do nothing. In this paper we look at these issues and propose a new framework. Our method uses a memory bank with a momentum encoder so small batches work fine, and we study which augmentations matter most. Experiments show our approach beats previous methods on several benchmarks while using less compute.
\section{Related Work}
Contrastive methods learn representations by attracting positive pairs and repelling negative pairs.
